  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for frost tonight inland from the\ncoast and north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSurface 1040mb high pressure is centered over Quebec this\nafternoon, with upper level high pressure building into the\nregion from Ontario. This pattern will continue tonight,\nresulting in clear skies and light winds, similar to last night.\nOnce again this should result in a cold night, frost, and river\nvalley fog. A modifying air mass could limit how low\ntemperatures fall tonight, but lows in the 30s are expected\noutside the Downeast Coast. Northern areas of Maine will likely\nreach freezing or below freezing. A Frost Advisory is in effect\nfor southern Penobscot County from midnight tonight to 7 AM \nThursday. While not as cold as tonight, Thursday night could see\nadditional patchy frost and fog, mainly in northern areas as \nclouds increase from the south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low will impact at least Downeast \nMaine late this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA coastal low is expected to develop across the Mid-Atlantic \nregion late this week while high pressure remains centered over \nQuebec. The timing and track of the ocean low is still uncertain\nwith blocking high pressure to the northwest of our forecast \narea and ridging across the Great Lakes. Latest ECMWF guidance \ndelays the onset of rain into the weekend as the blocking high \nremains resolute over Eastern Canada. This will result in \nadditional uncertainty in how close the low will pass to the \ncoast which will determine how far north the precipitation will \nreach. Regardless, northerly to northeasterly winds will become \ngusty over the weekend, especially along the coast, as a tight \npressure gradient forms. General trend currently is that the low\npressure system will continue to move southwards due to the \nhigh pressure, with majority of the rain staying over the waters\nand the Downeast region.",
